  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  A few strong thunderstorms are possible this afternoon and\n   evening. Main threats are intense rainfall rates and hail.\n\n-  Significant rain event for much of the Northern Rockies tonight\n   through Tuesday morning.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\n-  70 percent of ensemble solutions depict another weather-maker \n   to impact the Northern Rockies next weekend. \n\nA closed low pressure system is currently visible on satellite\nimagery along the Washington/ Oregon border. This features will\nshift eastwards into western Montana by early tomorrow morning and\ninto north central Montana by tomorrow evening.  \n\nAhead of the low this afternoon, waves of energy and moisture\ncontinue to be pumped into western Montana and north central\nIdaho, producing areas of showers and embedded thunderstorms.\nDynamic upper level forcing ahead of the low will continue to\nincrease through the late afternoon and evening, allowing for\nincreasing chances of thunderstorms, most likely between 3pm and\n9pm this evening. Upper level dynamics and wind shear will create\na favorable environment for a few strong to possibly severe \nthunderstorms, primarily across southwest Montana where less cloud\ncover has allowed for greater surface heating. The main threats \nwith storms will be intense rainfall rates of 0.75\" or more per \nhour, hail up to 1 inch in diameter and wind gusts up to 50 mph.\nStorm coverage may become more widespread through the evening as\nthe upper level low nudges eastwards. While flash flooding is a\nlow probability, flood prone areas and burn scars will be\nvulnerable if they take a direct hit from a stronger storm.\n\nThunderstorm coverage may lull briefly late this evening, but high\nresolution forecast models show another round developing around\nmidnight tonight in southern Idaho county, shifting into southwest\nand west central Montana early Monday morning. This next wave \nwill be enhanced by an incoming jet stream aloft and a passing \nmid- level cold front. Storms will again be capable of brief heavy\nrainfall, abundant lightning and even small hail.\n\nThe bulk of heavy precipitation with the low pressure system will\ncome Monday into early Tuesday morning. Widespread precipitation\nwill develop tomorrow morning in north central Idaho, shifting\ninto northwest Montana by Monday afternoon. The majority of the\nregion (other than far southwest Montana) have high chances (80 to\n100 percent) of seeing at least half an inch of rainfall. The one\nexception will be Lemhi County and far southwest Montana where\nlighter amounts are anticipated. Northwest Montana, especially\nnear the Continental Divide is still anticipated to received the\nheaviest amounts, with 1 to 3 inches of additional rainfall \nanticipated in the 24 hrs ending 6am Tuesday. Localized areas of 3\nto 4 inches are not out of the question. A flood watch has been\nissued to account for the potential of lowland flooding in poor\ndrainage areas, rapid rises on streams and creeks, possibly\nreaching bankfull, and debris fall on roadways. \n\nShowers will persist on Tuesday mainly across northwest Montana\nwhile other areas will start to moderate. Lingering boundary-layer\nmoisture will support overnight and early morning fog and low\nstratus clouds on Wednesday and possibly Thursday.  \n\nTemperatures will rebound Wednesday through Friday, getting back\nto near seasonal levels for this time of year...70s and 80s for\nthe lower elevations of Idaho. \n\nFor the longer range, including next weekend, a lot hinges on what\nevolves well upstream of us! One of the deepest troughs so far \nthis season across eastern Russia will support a very amplified \nridge over Alaska later this week. This will in turn support a \ndeepening trough over western Canada. This kind of setup can lead\nto another trough digging over the Pacific Northwest which could \nbring unsettled and cooler weather for us. Some of the guidance is \ndepicting a colder trough scenario which could bring lower snow \nlevels. In fact, the NBM is starting to show 20 to 30% chance for \nsnow above 7,000 feet along the Divide next Saturday!",
